<header><p class="st-kicker">colony · South America</p><h1 class="st-title">Governorate of New Castile</h1><p class="st-meta">1529 CE – 1542 CE · capital Lima</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The Spanish governorate granted to Francisco Pizarro, under which the conquest of the Inca Empire took place and Lima was founded.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>By the Capitulation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toledo">Toledo</a> in 1529, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-v-holy-roman-emperor">Charles V</a> named <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francisco-pizarro">Francisco Pizarro</a> governor and captain-general of New Castile, a strip extending 200 leagues south from the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/spanish-jamaica">Santiago</a> river in <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/republic-of-ecuador">Ecuador</a>. Pizarro sailed from <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/republic-of-panama">Panama</a> in 1530 and began the conquest of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/inca-empire">Inca Empire</a>, capturing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atahualpa">Atahualpa</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cajamarca">Cajamarca</a> in 1532.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>New Castile was governed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francisco-pizarro">Pizarro</a> with his brothers <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hernando-pizarro">Hernando</a>, Gonzalo and Juan. He founded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piura">San Miguel de Piura</a> in 1532 and the City of the Kings, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lima">Lima</a>, in 1535. Lands and native people were divided into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/encomienda">encomiendas</a> among the conquerors. The boundary with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diego-de-almagro">Diego de Almagro's</a> <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/governorate-of-new-toledo">Governorate of New Toledo</a>, especially the possession of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cusco">Cusco</a>, led to civil war, and Almagro was executed in 1538.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diego-de-almagro">Almagro</a>'s followers assassinated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francisco-pizarro">Pizarro</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lima">Lima</a> in 1541. In 1542 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-laws">New Laws</a> created the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/viceroyalty-of-peru">Viceroyalty of Peru</a>, which absorbed New Castile and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/governorate-of-new-toledo">New Toledo</a> under a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viceroy-of-india">viceroy</a>. Lima remained the capital of Spanish <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-america">South America</a> for nearly three centuries.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/governorate-of-new-castile.jpg" alt="Governorate of New Castile" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: Ningyou. · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Cross_of_Burgundy.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1529 CE – 1542 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>colony</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>colonial</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Lima</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Francisco Pizarro, Cristóbal Vaca de Castro</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Roman Catholicism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Spanish, Quechua</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/peru">Peru</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Nueva Castilla</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1536&amp;focus=governorate-of-new-castile" aria-label="Governorate of New Castile on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="2563 2627 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Governorate of New Castile lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M2883 2883L4910 989M2883 2883L2883 2883"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M4910 989h0M2883 2883h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M2883 2883h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M2883 2883h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Lima. <a href="/atlas?year=1536&amp;focus=governorate-of-new-castile">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
